ormer researchers, growing calls for statutory regulation and increased scrutiny from policymakers are all pointing towards the same underlying challenge: AI capability appears to be advancing faster than society’s ability to govern it.\n\n# The question we should be asking in the AI debate\n\n  * Soulla Kyriacou\n  * September 18, 2026\n  * AI, Human centred AI, Purpose\n\nRecent weeks have seen a notable shift in public discussion about artificial intelligence.\n\nWarnings from senior figures within leading AI companies, concerns raised by former researchers, growing calls for statutory regulation and increased scrutiny from policymakers are all pointing towards the same underlying challenge: AI capability appears to be advancing faster than society’s ability to govern it.\n\nAs a result, the debate is entering a new phase. Questions about innovation and adoption remain important, but they are increasingly being accompanied by deeper questions about accountability, oversight and the public interest.\n\nMuch of this discussion can be understood through a governance gap: a situation in which technological capabilities evolve more rapidly than the institutions, norms and frameworks designed to oversee them.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCurrent Events: September 2026 | Lead Stories (https://leadstories.com/current-events/2026/09/)\nciteturn895search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; ## Tuesday, September 15, 2026 ...   * Fact Check: FAKE Image of Trump Clearing Rubble at Ground Zero After 9/11 Is AI-Generated\n  * Fact Check: Parents Have NOT Gone Public To Complain About Child Deaths Under Lindsay Clancy's Nursing Care\n  * Fact Check: Dolly Parton's Family Did NOT Deny Taylor Swift Request To Headline Tribute Concert -- It Is Satire\n  * Fact Check: Gavin Newsom's Winery Did NOT Receive 'State Wildfire Payout' -- It Got A Private Settlement With Hundred Acre\n  * Fact Check: FAKE Video Of Truck Pushing Car From 